From 69e5a7f5ac82db70d326230d1f7eed49ee37516a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: agent-keys <noreply@agent-keys.invalid>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 11:56:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 15/20] fix(operator): a Safe without an ERC-8004 identity is
 handed over only after the Kernel proves it signs

For a Safe with an identity, `jinn signer-migrate` has the Kernel publish the attestation key
before the swap: an operation of the agent key under its mandate, and the proof that the Kernel
can act for the Safe it receives. Without an identity that step does not apply, and only the
mandate (the Kernel's code and AgentPolicy.isAllowed(Safe, execTransaction)) was checked before
the old EOA's swapOwner: the Kernel was never asked to sign. If the agent's key or backend could
not sign, the Safe ended with an owner that cannot act for it. The internal audit of
2026-11-28, finding AK-2026-11-28-01 (Medium).

- signer/migrate.ts: a new step, `agent-proof`, after `mandate-admits-safe`. For a Safe without
  an identity that is still to be handed over, the operator passes the userOpHash of an
  operation of the Kernel signed with the agent permission (`--agent-proof`). checkAgentProof
  reads the EntryPoint's (v0.7) UserOperationEvent for it, from the last 3600 blocks, and
  requires: the Kernel as its sender; `success`; a nonce whose key names the agent permission
  (Kernel v3.3: mode 0, validation type 0x02 and the permission id in the nonce's upper 48 bits,
  as agent-signer builds it; the owner's root path is key 0 and is refused); and a block at most
  an hour old by its timestamp (AGENT_PROOF_MAX_AGE_SECONDS). An hour leaves time to send the
  operation, read the dry run and run the migration, and not to bring one from before a change
  of the key, the backend or the mandate. Without such a proof the handover is refused, in a dry
  run too, with nothing changed, and the error says how to make one: an operation the agent's
  mandate allows, sent with `agent-signer op send` (no --root). A re-run that finds the Safe
  handed over needs no proof (`not-applicable`); a Safe with an identity keeps its path, where
  the attestation key is the proof (`not-applicable`, "the Kernel signs in attestation-key").
- signer/kernel-safe-broadcaster.ts: USER_OPERATION_EVENT is exported for that reading.
- cli/commands/signer-migrate.ts: `--agent-proof <userOpHash>` (0x and 64 hex digits), passed to
  every Safe's migration; the help says when it is needed.
- tests: unit, the regression the review asked for, mirroring the identity path's: no identity
  and a Kernel that cannot sign means swapOwner is never sent, in a run and in a dry run. A proof
  is refused when its event is not found or is outside the 3600 blocks, when another account sent
  it, when it reverted, when its nonce key is the root path's, another permission's or the
  enable mode's, and when it is 3601 s old; at 3600 s it passes and the Safe changes hands; a
  re-run needs none. On Safe 1.3.0's own bytecode (test/hermetic/signer-migrate-refusals.test.ts),
  with a stand-in at the EntryPoint's address that emits the event as given: refused without a
  proof, for an unknown hash, another sender, a reverted operation, the root path and an
  operation older than an hour (evm_increaseTime); a fresh one passes. In the full loop
  (full-loop-kernel-owner.test.ts), on agent-signer's real operations: the owner's root grant is
  refused as a proof, the agent's attestation-key operation passes. Each rule was broken once on
  purpose and a test caught it (no proof accepted, the nonce key, the age, the sender, success,
  the step skipped).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
